{"affiliation": "Department of Biochemistry and National Centre for Biomedical Engineering Science, National University of Ireland, University Road, Galway, Ireland."}]}}, {"fullName": "Logue SE", "lastName": "Logue", "firstName": "Susan E", "initials": "SE", "authorId": {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0001-7938-3558"}}, {"fullName": "Gorman AM", "lastName": "Gorman", "firstName": "Adrienne M", "initials": "AM", "authorId": {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-6068-0058"}}, {"fullName": "Samali A", "lastName": "Samali", "firstName": "Afshin", "initials": "A", "authorId": {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-8610-8375"}}]}, "authorIdList": {"authorId": [{"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0001-7938-3558"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-5708-3535"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-6068-0058"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-8610-8375"}]}, "dataLinksTagsList": {"dataLinkstag": ["altmetrics"]}, "journalInfo": {"issue": "9", "volume": "7", "journalIssueId": 1346704, "dateOfPublication": "2006 Sep", "monthOfPublication": 9, "yearOfPublication": 2006, "journal": {"medlineAbbreviation": "EMBO Rep", "title": "EMBO reports", "essn": "1469-3178", "issn": "1469-221X", "nlmid": "100963049", "isoabbreviation": "EMBO Rep"}, "printPublicationDate": "2006-09-01"}, "pubYear": "2006", "pageInfo": "880-885", "abstractText": "The efficient functioning of the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) is essential for most cellular activities and survival. Conditions that interfere with ER function lead to the accumulation and aggregation of unfolded proteins. ER transmembrane receptors detect the onset of ER stress and initiate the unfolded protein response (UPR) to restore normal ER function. If the stress is prolonged, or the adaptive response fails, apoptotic cell death ensues. Many studies have focused on how this failure initiates apoptosis, as ER stress-induced apoptosis is implicated in the pathophysiology of several neurodegenerative and cardiovascular diseases. In this review, we examine the role of the molecules that are activated during the UPR in order to identify the molecular switch from the adaptive phase to apoptosis. We discuss how the activation of these molecules leads to the commitment of death and the mechanisms that are responsible for the final demise of the cell.", "affiliation": "Department of Biochemistry and National Centre for Biomedical Engineering Science, National University of Ireland, University Road, Galway, Ireland.", "publicationStatus": "ppublish", "language": "eng", "pubModel": "Print", "pubTypeList": {"pubType": ["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't", "review-article", "Review", "Journal Article"]}, "meshHeadingList": {"meshHeading": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Endoplasmic Reticulum", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "abbreviation": "PH", "qualifierName": "physiology"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Animals"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Humans"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Mice"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Molecular Chaperones"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "descriptorName": "Signal Transduction"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "descriptorName": "Apoptosis"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Protein Folding"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Protein Transport"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Oxidative Stress"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Models, Biological"}]}, "chemicalList": {"chemical": [{"name": "Molecular Chaperones", "registryNumber": "0"}]}, "subsetList": {"subset": [{"code": "IM", "name": "Index Medicus"}]}, "fullTextUrlList": {"fullTextUrl": [{"availability": "Subscription required", "availabilityCode": "S", "documentStyle": "doi", "site": "DOI", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1038/sj.embor.7400779"}, {"availability": "Free", "availabilityCode": "F", "documentStyle": "html", "site": "Europe_PMC", "url": "https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC1559676"}, {"availability":